<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mariano Gálvez</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1794">1794</a>–<a href="/years/1862">1862</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mariano Gálvez was a Guatemalan lawyer and liberal politician, head of state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> within the Central American Federation from 1831 to 1838. He introduced civil marriage, the Livingston penal codes, secular education and land grants to foreign colonists. A cholera epidemic and a head tax provoked the rural revolt led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera</a> that brought him down.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mariano-galvez.jpg" alt="Mariano Gálvez" width="199" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mariano_G%C3%A1lvez.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gálvez was a foundling, left in a basket at the house of Fray Toribio Carvajal, who gave him for adoption to the wealthy family of Gertrudis Gálvez, whose name he took. He studied at the convent school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a> and then law at the Royal and Pontifical University of San Carlos Borromeo, receiving a doctorate on 16 December 1819. He counselled Gabino Gaínza, favoured the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> after independence, and in 1825 presided over the first federal Congress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>. In the civil war of 1826 he sided with the Federalists and joined the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-morazan">Francisco Morazán</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Chief of state from 28 August 1831 to 3 March 1838, Gálvez governed after the expulsion of the conservative Aycinena family and the regular clergy in 1829. Liberal historians such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-rosa">Ramón Rosa</a> and Lorenzo Montúfar y Rivera credit him with making public instruction independent of the Church, ending religious festivals as holidays, founding the National Library and the National Museum and guaranteeing freedom of the press and thought. He also reformed the courts and municipal government and imposed a general head tax that bore heavily on the native population.</p><p>On 6 August 1834 he contracted with Marshall Bennett, Morazán's partner in the fine wood business, to colonise Izabal, las Verapaces, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peten">Petén</a> and Belize within twenty years, which proved impossible. Re-elected in February 1835, he faced an epidemic of Asiatic cholera that clergy blamed on the government poisoning the springs, and from 1837 peasant revolts against the heretics spread in the name of the true religion. His scorched-earth repression of rural communities turned former friends such as José Francisco Barrundia and Pedro Molina against him. In February 1838 the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera</a> entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a> and he gave up power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Gálvez died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> on 29 March 1862 and was buried in the Cemetery of San Fernando. His remains were brought back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> in 1925 and placed in the old School of Law in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>. The Universidad Mariano Gálvez de Guatemala, founded in 1966, is named after him, and in 1836 he had been elected a member of the American Philosophical Society.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mariano Gálvez” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Morazán, elected president in 1830, and <b>Mariano Gálvez</b>, head of the State of Guatemala from 1831, pursued liberal programmes: the closing of monasteries, the end of compulsory tithes, civil marriage and divorce, religious freedom, public education, land grants to foreign colonisation companies and, in Guatemala, the Livingston Codes,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Liberal reforms under the state governor <b>Mariano Gálvez</b> provoked a peasant revolt in 1837, inflamed by a cholera epidemic and by attacks on the church, which brought the young mestizo leader Rafael Carrera to prominence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Republic of Guatemala · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mariano Gálvez</b>, head of state from 1831, introduced civil marriage, the Livingston penal codes, land grants to foreign colonists and a head tax.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-guatemala">State of Guatemala</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=14.64,-90.51&amp;year=1838" aria-label="Guatemala City on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2075 1619 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mariano Gálvez"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2525 1889h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2525 1889h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=14.64,-90.51&amp;year=1838">What was here in 1838? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
